atlas:index/emailProvider:EmailProvider.description:
## #<span pulumi-lang-nodejs=" atlas.EmailProvider
" pulumi-lang-dotnet=" atlas.EmailProvider
" pulumi-lang-go=" EmailProvider
" pulumi-lang-python=" EmailProvider
" pulumi-lang-yaml=" atlas.EmailProvider
" pulumi-lang-java=" atlas.EmailProvider
" pulumi-lang-hcl=" atlas_email_provider
"> atlas.EmailProvider
</span>
Your own email provider (bring your own account), so Atlas sends sign-in codes,
magic links and notifications through it instead of the shared default sender.

Set **exactly one** transport block: <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`resend`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Resend`" pulumi-lang-go="`resend`" pulumi-lang-python="`resend`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`resend`" pulumi-lang-java="`resend`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`resend`">`resend`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`ses`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Ses`" pulumi-lang-go="`ses`" pulumi-lang-python="`ses`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`ses`" pulumi-lang-java="`ses`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`ses`">`ses`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`smtp`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Smtp`" pulumi-lang-go="`smtp`" pulumi-lang-python="`smtp`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`smtp`" pulumi-lang-java="`smtp`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`smtp`">`smtp`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`brevo`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Brevo`" pulumi-lang-go="`brevo`" pulumi-lang-python="`brevo`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`brevo`" pulumi-lang-java="`brevo`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`brevo`">`brevo`</span> or
<span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`mailgun`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Mailgun`" pulumi-lang-go="`mailgun`" pulumi-lang-python="`mailgun`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`mailgun`" pulumi-lang-java="`mailgun`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`mailgun`">`mailgun`</span>. Secret settings (API keys, passwords) are **sensitive and
write-only**: Atlas stores them encrypted and never returns them, so they are
kept from your configuration; <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`secretsSet`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`SecretsSet`" pulumi-lang-go="`secretsSet`" pulumi-lang-python="`secrets_set`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`secretsSet`" pulumi-lang-java="`secretsSet`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`secrets_set`">`secretsSet`</span> reports which ones Atlas holds.
Non-secret settings are read back, so drift shows in the plan.

Removing an optional non-secret setting from a block keeps the stored value;
switch transport (or destroy and re-create) to reset it. Destroying the
resource returns the channel to the Atlas default sender.

When this provider is the only way the instance can send email and
email-dependent features are on (email code or email link sign-in, sign-up
email verification, password reset), Atlas refuses to remove it
(`409 EMAIL_PROVIDER_IN_USE`) and the destroy fails, naming those features.
