## #<span pulumi-lang-nodejs=" atlas.IdApp
" pulumi-lang-dotnet=" atlas.IdApp
" pulumi-lang-go=" IdApp
" pulumi-lang-python=" IdApp
" pulumi-lang-yaml=" atlas.IdApp
" pulumi-lang-java=" atlas.IdApp
" pulumi-lang-hcl=" atlas_id_app
"> atlas.IdApp
</span>
A **Sign in with Atlas** app: an OpenID Connect client on the shared Atlas
issuer (`https://atlasauth.net`), registered to your platform account. Any site
can then offer "Sign in with Atlas" using the app's <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`clientId`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`ClientId`" pulumi-lang-go="`clientId`" pulumi-lang-python="`client_id`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`clientId`" pulumi-lang-java="`clientId`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`client_id`">`clientId`</span>.

This resource uses the platform surface, so the provider needs a **platform
key** (`psk_...`) — set <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`platformKey`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`PlatformKey`" pulumi-lang-go="`platformKey`" pulumi-lang-python="`platform_key`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`platformKey`" pulumi-lang-java="`platformKey`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`platform_key`">`platformKey`</span> in the provider block or
`ATLAS_PLATFORM_KEY` in the environment.

A confidential app's <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`clientSecret`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`ClientSecret`" pulumi-lang-go="`clientSecret`" pulumi-lang-python="`client_secret`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`clientSecret`" pulumi-lang-java="`clientSecret`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`client_secret`">`clientSecret`</span> is returned once, on create (and again on
each rotation), and kept as a sensitive value in state. Change
<span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`rotateSecretTrigger`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`RotateSecretTrigger`" pulumi-lang-go="`rotateSecretTrigger`" pulumi-lang-python="`rotate_secret_trigger`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`rotateSecretTrigger`" pulumi-lang-java="`rotateSecretTrigger`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`rotate_secret_trigger`">`rotateSecretTrigger`</span> to rotate it in place; the previous secret keeps working
for 24 hours.

<span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`branding`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Branding`" pulumi-lang-go="`branding`" pulumi-lang-python="`branding`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`branding`" pulumi-lang-java="`branding`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`branding`">`branding`</span> is a JSON object (write it with <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-go="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-python="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-java="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`jsonencode`">`jsonencode`</span>). It is compared
**semantically**: the key order, formatting and <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`null`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Null`" pulumi-lang-go="`null`" pulumi-lang-python="`null`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`null`" pulumi-lang-java="`null`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`null`">`null`</span> members of what Atlas
returns never cause drift. (Editing only the whitespace or key order of a hand-written JSON string still shows one
in-place update, because Terraform compares configuration text; <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-go="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-python="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-java="`jsonencode`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`jsonencode`">`jsonencode`</span> output
is canonical, so it never does.)
A key you remove from the configuration is cleared on the server.

API: `POST /v1/platform/atlas_id_apps`, `GET`/`PATCH`/`DELETE
/v1/platform/atlas_id_apps/:id`, `POST /v1/platform/atlas_id_apps/:id/rotate_secret`.

## Example Usage

<!--Start PulumiCodeChooser -->
```typescript
