</span>
Your own SMS provider (bring your own account), so Atlas sends phone codes
through it instead of the shared default sender.

Set **exactly one** transport block (a nested attribute, `twilio = { ... }`):
<span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`twilio`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Twilio`" pulumi-lang-go="`twilio`" pulumi-lang-python="`twilio`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`twilio`" pulumi-lang-java="`twilio`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`twilio`">`twilio`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`vonage`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Vonage`" pulumi-lang-go="`vonage`" pulumi-lang-python="`vonage`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`vonage`" pulumi-lang-java="`vonage`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`vonage`">`vonage`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`messagebird`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Messagebird`" pulumi-lang-go="`messagebird`" pulumi-lang-python="`messagebird`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`messagebird`" pulumi-lang-java="`messagebird`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`messagebird`">`messagebird`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`plivo`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Plivo`" pulumi-lang-go="`plivo`" pulumi-lang-python="`plivo`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`plivo`" pulumi-lang-java="`plivo`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`plivo`">`plivo`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`telnyx`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Telnyx`" pulumi-lang-go="`telnyx`" pulumi-lang-python="`telnyx`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`telnyx`" pulumi-lang-java="`telnyx`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`telnyx`">`telnyx`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`sinch`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Sinch`" pulumi-lang-go="`sinch`" pulumi-lang-python="`sinch`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`sinch`" pulumi-lang-java="`sinch`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`sinch`">`sinch`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`infobip`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Infobip`" pulumi-lang-go="`infobip`" pulumi-lang-python="`infobip`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`infobip`" pulumi-lang-java="`infobip`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`infobip`">`infobip`</span>,
<span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`clicksend`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Clicksend`" pulumi-lang-go="`clicksend`" pulumi-lang-python="`clicksend`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`clicksend`" pulumi-lang-java="`clicksend`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`clicksend`">`clicksend`</span>, <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`bandwidth`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Bandwidth`" pulumi-lang-go="`bandwidth`" pulumi-lang-python="`bandwidth`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`bandwidth`" pulumi-lang-java="`bandwidth`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`bandwidth`">`bandwidth`</span> or <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`awsSns`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`AwsSns`" pulumi-lang-go="`awsSns`" pulumi-lang-python="`aws_sns`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`awsSns`" pulumi-lang-java="`awsSns`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`aws_sns`">`awsSns`</span>. Secret settings are **sensitive and
write-only**: Atlas stores them encrypted and never returns them, so they are
kept from your configuration; <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`secretsSet`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`SecretsSet`" pulumi-lang-go="`secretsSet`" pulumi-lang-python="`secrets_set`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`secretsSet`" pulumi-lang-java="`secretsSet`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`secrets_set`">`secretsSet`</span> reports which ones Atlas holds.
Non-secret settings are read back. Destroying the resource returns the channel
to the Atlas default sender.

